    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/493295874/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """her mother-in-law, who never would accept their marriage as a fact, but kept attributing every fault in Bryson to “his unfortunate marriage”. Even his admiration for olive trees she set down as a “morbid, unnatural taste” - “such ugly, gnarled things!” - due to the degeneration caused by Edith’s baleful influence.
Edith said that much as she loved Bryson, and thoroughly congenial as they were, if he brought his mother to live with them, she would just leave. 
Bernhard dined with the Eyres. 
Thursday, Apr. 9, 1896, Fiesole 
Edith and Bryson went off to Siena.
Mr. and Mrs. Benn came to lunch, and we had the usual struggle with sleepiness afterwards, keeping """^^xsd:string ;
